{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1300687-2e-tuatha-pantheon-signature-power-what-s-the-upside","id":1300687,"title":"[2E] Tuatha Pantheon Signature Power... What's the Upside?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-03-28T15:24:15-07:00","id":1300687,"title":"[2E] Tuatha Pantheon Signature Power... What's the Upside?","content":"Looking at the Tuatha signature pantheon power, is it just me or did the Tuatha get a bad deal on their signature? Being saddled with a geas seems like a big negative in that it only gives a little bit of momentum, and if it does end up being broken it really shuts you down. Most of the other pantheons get little benefits in line with regular pantheon powers, but it seems like the Tuatha just get a bad condition that doesn't really pay much back in the way of momentum compared to the possible penalty.<br /> <br /> Plus, Lay Geis seems like a real bad power as well. It sounds nice (too nice, even - you could theoretically lay a geis prohibiting combat on any enemy that doesn't immediately start swinging at you as soon as they're aware you're in the room), but mechanically I'm not sure how it's supposed to work on Storyteller characters. A geis, if broken, makes the target lose all Legend and ends all Legend based effects... but Storyteller characters don't have any mechanics based off of Legend. So what's the penalty for them breaking one of your geasa?<br /> <br /> Tongue of the Bard is good, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2433-highpriest","name":"HighPriest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2433_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T17:43:35-07:00","id":1300715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HighPriest</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1300687-2e-tuatha-pantheon-signature-power-what-s-the-upside#post1300687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Looking at the Tuatha signature pantheon power, is it just me or did the Tuatha get a bad deal on their signature? Being saddled with a geas seems like a big negative in that it only gives a little bit of momentum, and if it does end up being broken it really shuts you down. Most of the other pantheons get little benefits in line with regular pantheon powers, but it seems like the Tuatha just get a bad condition that doesn't really pay much back in the way of momentum compared to the possible penalty.<br /> <br /> Plus, Lay Geis seems like a real bad power as well. It sounds nice (too nice, even - you could theoretically lay a geis prohibiting combat on any enemy that doesn't immediately start swinging at you as soon as they're aware you're in the room), but mechanically I'm not sure how it's supposed to work on Storyteller characters. A geis, if broken, makes the target lose all Legend and ends all Legend based effects... but Storyteller characters don't have any mechanics based off of Legend. So what's the penalty for them breaking one of your geasa?<br /> <br /> Tongue of the Bard is good, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can talk about this on a few fronts, though I do agree that Lay Geis isn't... the best power for several reasons I'll get into.<br /> <br /> But, the Innate Power is rather honest to the Irish Sagas. A Geis is not supposed to be helpful in the slightest, it is a collar fitted to the necks of the mighty to prevent them running ramshod over society (this is a scholarly theory, we don't actually have any solid answer on what the purpose of these were except who had them and who didn't and why) and functioning as a method for these supernatural individuals to be controlled if necessary. It is a Innate Power that has to exist for the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Danann to make sense as a Pantheon within the context of their culture and literary record, but it does suck from an in-game perspective. From an out-of-game perspective I love it both as a Storyteller and a Player as it's a great vehicle for the narrative, but it doesn't pack quite the punch as some of the others.<br /> <br /> Sadly, there is no way to make having a Geas a good thing beyond the meta-resource of momentum generating. Scion 1e presented them as things that made you more powerful which was just a particularly imaginative lie, and to be honest to the culture it just has to be a pure drawback. It <i>does</i> give the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Scions a good boast against other Scions though since they are effectively playing the game of life on a harder 'difficulty' and that's something they would find an inordinate amount of pride in.<br /> <br /> Lay Geis as a Boon doesn't... it has problems both on the mechanical side that you pointed out, but also on the cultural side. Lay Geis becomes useful when you abuse it, but that is <i>entirely </i>inappropriate and absolutely beyond the pale for what they should be used for. From a purely mechanical perspective, it's a <i>great</i> assassination tool since it can thrust mortals into live-or-die situations, but that's... not... what a Geis is for. And, if someone was approaching the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 from the same desire for a respectful depiction as we see with the other Pantheons in Scion: Hero, abusing Lay Geis (using it, essentially, for your own benefit) would put you on a 'immediately kill, don't let them speak' list for the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 similar to someone using satire unjustly. <br /> <br /> Now, there are two instances of someone putting a Geis on someone else to force them to act in a specific way, Gr\u00e1inne puts one on Diarmuind to force him to run off with her, and Derdriu does the same to Noise for the same reason. (In both of these cases a Geis isn't explicitly stated but it's pretty clear that's what's going on) This could be an... interesting, but exceptionally morally questionable Power for a Scion in it's a tool that forces people to do things they don't want to do because you are threatening their life and honour. However, as you pointed out, mechanically the Boon doesn't <i>actually</i> do that.<br /> <br /> Geis is a Purview that I would have handled very differently, I'm... very unsure how it can grow into Demigod and God Tiers as it is taking a Geis-central approach to the Purview rather than seeing the Geis as sort of an auxiliary feature of being a supernatural person in Irish saga lit. as, you know, they actually are. I probably would have made a PsP about being really weird about Callings or something instead since the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Danann are <i>vastly</i> more defined by their Callings than their Purviews, as their Purviews are just expressions of the supernatural height of their professional talents (see: Cath Maige Tuired, the Training of C\u00fa Chulainn, etc) and their professions are essentially just their Callings from a different angle.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T22:27:20-07:00","id":1301034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1300687-2e-tuatha-pantheon-signature-power-what-s-the-upside?p=1300715#post1300715\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I can talk about this on a few fronts, though I do agree that Lay Geis isn't... the best power for several reasons I'll get into.<br /> <br /> But, the Innate Power is rather honest to the Irish Sagas. A Geis is not supposed to be helpful in the slightest, it is a collar fitted to the necks of the mighty to prevent them running ramshod over society (this is a scholarly theory, we don't actually have any solid answer on what the purpose of these were except who had them and who didn't and why) and functioning as a method for these supernatural individuals to be controlled if necessary. It is a Innate Power that has to exist for the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Danann to make sense as a Pantheon within the context of their culture and literary record, but it does suck from an in-game perspective. From an out-of-game perspective I love it both as a Storyteller and a Player as it's a great vehicle for the narrative, but it doesn't pack quite the punch as some of the others.<br /> <br /> Sadly, there is no way to make having a Geas a good thing beyond the meta-resource of momentum generating. Scion 1e presented them as things that made you more powerful which was just a particularly imaginative lie, and to be honest to the culture it just has to be a pure drawback. It <i>does</i> give the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Scions a good boast against other Scions though since they are effectively playing the game of life on a harder 'difficulty' and that's something they would find an inordinate amount of pride in.<br /> <br /> Lay Geis as a Boon doesn't... it has problems both on the mechanical side that you pointed out, but also on the cultural side. Lay Geis becomes useful when you abuse it, but that is <i>entirely </i>inappropriate and absolutely beyond the pale for what they should be used for. From a purely mechanical perspective, it's a <i>great</i> assassination tool since it can thrust mortals into live-or-die situations, but that's... not... what a Geis is for. And, if someone was approaching the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 from the same desire for a respectful depiction as we see with the other Pantheons in Scion: Hero, abusing Lay Geis (using it, essentially, for your own benefit) would put you on a 'immediately kill, don't let them speak' list for the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 similar to someone using satire unjustly.<br /> <br /> Now, there are two instances of someone putting a Geis on someone else to force them to act in a specific way, Gr\u00e1inne puts one on Diarmuind to force him to run off with her, and Derdriu does the same to Noise for the same reason. (In both of these cases a Geis isn't explicitly stated but it's pretty clear that's what's going on) This could be an... interesting, but exceptionally morally questionable Power for a Scion in it's a tool that forces people to do things they don't want to do because you are threatening their life and honour. However, as you pointed out, mechanically the Boon doesn't <i>actually</i> do that.<br /> <br /> Geis is a Purview that I would have handled very differently, I'm... very unsure how it can grow into Demigod and God Tiers as it is taking a Geis-central approach to the Purview rather than seeing the Geis as sort of an auxiliary feature of being a supernatural person in Irish saga lit. as, you know, they actually are. I probably would have made a PsP about being really weird about Callings or something instead since the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Danann are <i>vastly</i> more defined by their Callings than their Purviews, as their Purviews are just expressions of the supernatural height of their professional talents (see: Cath Maige Tuired, the Training of C\u00fa Chulainn, etc) and their professions are essentially just their Callings from a different angle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the response, Watcher. I don't mind having a geas, but it feels like it's the only basic pantheon power with such a large mechanical downside, beyond being a somewhat boring momentum generator.<br /> <br /> It's interesting to hear how problematic using the Lay Geis ability would be in the context of the pantheon, besides being mechanically busted. That issue is easy enough to avoid by just not taking that boon - characters get so few that not selecting that one is easy enough.<br /> <br /> At the moment, I'm think I'll ask the ST if we can work out some sort of alternate default power for the Tuatha PSP, or at least beefing it up to the level where it's in line with, say, the Norse one - mechanically another sort of &quot;meh&quot; momentum generator in my mind, but one that is less likely to leave the character totally drained of Legend in worst case situations. Not sure what that would be yet, but I'll think on your last paragraph about how something Calling-centric might be appropriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2433-highpriest","name":"HighPriest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2433_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}